# Kconfig for NoMount

config NOMOUNT
	tristate "NoMount Path Redirection Subsystem"
	default n
	help
	  NoMount allows path redirection and virtual file injection
	  without mounting filesystems. Useful for systemless modifications.

	  NOTE: on this tree (Linux 4.9) the option is intentionally
	  default n and must be enabled explicitly in the defconfig.

config NOMOUNT_DEFAULT_ON
	bool "Arm NoMount at boot"
	depends on NOMOUNT
	default n
	help
	  When set, the subsystem starts honouring rules as soon as the
	  key type is registered.

	  When left unset (the default, and strongly recommended), the
	  subsystem is completely inert: no VFS hook is installed and no
	  rule is accepted until the master switch is flipped at runtime:

	    nomount_enabled=1                                  (kernel cmdline)
	    echo 1 > /sys/module/nomount/parameters/nomount_enabled
	    add_key ... nomount <NM_CMD_SET_ENABLED payload>   (keyring)

	  Keeping this off is the boot-safety net: a broken or unfinished
	  port can never turn into a bootloop because nothing is patched
	  into the VFS until somebody asks for it.
